---
import Layout from "../layouts/BasicLayout.astro";
import Card from "../components/Card/index.astro";
import Career from "../components/Career.astro";
import Goal from "../components/Goal.astro";
import IntroCard from "../components/IntroCard.astro";
import AboutMe from "../components/AboutMe.astro";
import Now from "../components/Now.astro";
import {DESIGN_PAGE} from "../content/design";
import {FOOTER, METADATA} from "../content/site";
import {GOALS} from "../lib/goal";
import {formatDateline, UPDATED_AT} from "../lib/projection";
import {iconClass} from "../lib/icons";

/*
 * THE DATELINE RENDERS ONCE, HERE, AND NOT ON THE GOAL CARDS. One timestamp covers
 * both sports, so two copies could only ever disagree or repeat.
 *
 * It reads the bot's own `updated_at` rather than a build-time date, and the reason
 * is not determinism alone. The workflow commits only when the kilometres actually
 * move (`git diff --quiet` in .github/workflows/strava-progress.yml is what stops a
 * deploy every night), so a build-time date would claim the figures were checked
 * today when the deploy that produced them may be a week old. The stamp means "the
 * day the kilometres last MOVED", which is what "Updated" should mean, and three
 * flat days correctly leave it three days old.
 */
const dateline = formatDateline();

/*
 * THE HEART STANDS IN FOR A WORD, so losing it loses meaning rather than decoration — the
 * sentence reads "Built with <heart>", and the `sr-only` "love" beside it is what a listener
 * gets. In forced colours it was painting rgb(255,255,255) on a card of rgb(255,255,255):
 * measured 1.00:1, the glyph simply absent, leaving "Built with , more love to Astro template
 * by Gianmarco" on screen. The shared rule in BasicLayout.astro paints it, and paints it
 * `CanvasText` — neither a link nor a control but body copy, which is what the mode reserves
 * that colour for. It takes that by being inside neither an anchor nor a button, so this page
 * declares nothing for it and needs no class hook to be found by.
 */

/*
 * The footer heart is toned from a theme token, and the token sits on a WRAPPER
 * around the glyph rather than on the glyph itself. That is not decoration — it is
 * the only arrangement that does not depend on the order UnoCSS happens to emit
 * two rules in.
 *
 * An icon here is a mask box, and presetIcons gives its rule two declarations that
 * matter: it paints with the current colour, and it sets the colour to the
 * inherited one. Put a colour utility on the SAME element and the two rules both
 * set colour at equal specificity — one class each — so the winner is whichever
 * lands later in the stylesheet. It does today, by about nine kilobytes, and the
 * heart renders correctly; nothing would notice if a future UnoCSS emitted them the
 * other way round, and the glyph would silently go back to the body text colour with
 * every test still green.
 *
 * On an ancestor there is no contest at all: the glyph's own rule inherits, which is
 * exactly what it asks to do, and it inherits the token. ProgressBar.astro already
 * does this — the ink token is on the fill, not on the icon inside it — so this is
 * the established shape here rather than a new one.
 *
 * ---
 *
 * THE RIGHT-HAND STACK'S COST BELOW lg. The wrapper below carries `contents` there,
 * so it generates no box and its three children are direct grid items again — but a
 * wrapper still changes SIBLING ORDER, and auto-placement is driven by DOM order.
 * These three cards were not contiguous siblings before, so wrapping them moves Now
 * ahead of about-me and both role cards at every width below lg. "The smaller
 * layouts are untouched" was the claim here and it is false; this is what was
 * measured instead, with card sizing held constant (this build, wrapper removed from
 * the DOM, so the wrapper is the only variable):
 *
 *   md 768x900 — five cards re-place. Now (390,885) -> (24,583); about-me x=24 ->
 *   x=390; the HeyMax card (390,583) -> (24,745); the NCS card x=24 -> x=390; and
 *   the FOOTER leaves the right column for the left one, x=390 -> x=24. Page height
 *   1165 -> 1191. The same reshuffle at 1023x900 (footer x=518 -> x=24).
 *
 *   390x844, one column — Now moves from seventh card to fourth, 670px up the page.
 *
 * Reading order moves with placement below lg, so the two still agree there; that
 * re-ordering is the trade accepted for the stack. At lg they do NOT agree: Now is
 * read before about-me and both role cards while being painted 76px below them.
 * If the old sub-lg placement ever has to be restored, the fix is explicit `md:`
 * placement tokens on Now/AboutMe/Career/the footer card, decoupling placement from
 * DOM order — not the removal of this wrapper, which lg needs.
 *
 * ---
 *
 * THE HEIGHT BUDGET BELOW IS A FLOOR, NOT A CLAMP, AND THE ROWS SIZE TO THEIR
 * CONTENT. Between them that is the whole of what lets this page stop being
 * single-screen when the text inside it grows, by whatever means it grew.
 *
 * `main` used to be clamped between a floor and a CAP around a viewport height, and
 * both ends used to be absolute — 736px and 800px. Absolute was the first defect:
 * the grid's height could not move while every card's contents scaled with the root
 * font-size, and every card clips, so the difference was not pushed below the fold,
 * it was deleted. Measured as ink past each card's clip edge summed over the page, at
 * 1024x800: 4.8px lost at a 17px root, 196 at 18, 457 at 20, 966 at 22, 1596 at 24.
 * Writing both ends in `rem` closed that, because the reader's zoom moves the root
 * font-size and a rem budget moves with it.
 *
 * THE CAP ITSELF WAS THE SECOND DEFECT, and no unit could have closed it. WCAG SC
 * 1.4.12 lets a reader set line-height, letter-spacing, word-spacing and paragraph
 * spacing directly; none of those touches the root font-size, so the type inside the
 * cards grows while every font-relative length on this page — the budget, the
 * breakpoints, the card padding — stays exactly where it was. The grid could not
 * follow, and the cards clipped. Measured on the revision before this one, ink past
 * each card's clip edge with the criterion's four metrics applied: 1157.7px deleted at
 * 1024x600, 850.7 at 1024x768, and 63 at 1440x900 and every larger viewport. All of it
 * on the BOTTOM edge; the right edge was 0 everywhere, because a card grows vertically
 * with its content and never horizontally.
 *
 * So the cap is gone, `min-height` carries the whole clamp on its own, and the row
 * template is `min-content` rather than eight equal fractions. THE TWO HALVES DO
 * DIFFERENT JOBS, and each was measured alone against a real build.
 *
 * Removing the cap is what closes SC 1.4.12, and it closes it on its own: with the
 * eight-fraction template left exactly as it was, the loss is 0px at 1024x600,
 * 1024x768, 1280x800, 1440x900 and 1920x1080. A fraction track sums to its container
 * only while that container has a DEFINITE height; once `min-height` is the only
 * constraint the height is indefinite, so each track resolves against its own content
 * contribution and grows. Measured on that build at 1440x900: eight tracks of 90px
 * with the criterion off, eight of 136px with it on.
 *
 * Content-sized rows without freeing the cap are still WORSE than shipping neither,
 * because the tracks then overflow a container that still clips.
 *
 * What the content-sized template buys is the DEFAULT-size page, not the criterion.
 * Under the freed floor the eight fraction tracks all round up to the tallest one, so
 * the grid asks for far more room than its content needs: this branch with the
 * fraction template restored measures 810px at the default text size, and the same
 * change made on the previous revision alone measures 912px, against 797px as
 * shipped. At 1280x800 that is the difference between fitting and scrolling. Both
 * halves are kept, and together it is 0px lost at every viewport.
 *
 * WHAT IT COSTS A READER WHO CHANGES NOTHING. The grid now asks for 797px where it was
 * given 800, so from a 797px-tall viewport up nothing scrolls that did not scroll
 * before. Below that the page grows: at 1024x768 and 1366x768 the document goes 768 to
 * 797px and gains a 29px scroll where it used to fit exactly, by squeezing every row
 * 5px and eating the footer card's bottom padding. BE PRECISE ABOUT WHAT SCROLLS:
 * measured at both of those viewports, the text ink below the fold is ZERO. The 29px
 * is 5px of the footer card's own box plus `main`'s bottom padding, so the cost is a
 * scrollbar, not a line of copy. "The page stops fitting" is the wrong summary of that —
 * it reads as content going under the fold, and overstates it. Rect-diffed against a build of the
 * previous revision across ten lg viewports: no card moves horizontally or changes
 * width, the intro card gains 5px, the footer 14px — that is its own padding coming
 * back — and everything below the first row shifts down 5px.
 *
 * The 50rem term survives inside the floor, and it is doing a different job there: it
 * caps how tall the grid STRETCHES on a tall viewport, not how tall it may GROW. The
 * `vh` term in the middle stays for the reason it always did — it is what makes the
 * page single-screen at all, and a flat 50rem floor would push a 760px-tall viewport
 * into scrolling at the DEFAULT text size, which is a regression rather than a fix.
 *
 * The budget is only half of it. The other half is that the breakpoints are
 * text-relative too, for the reason in uno.config.ts — freeing the height alone still
 * left 846px of ink lost at 1024x800 and a 24px root, because four columns of ~10em
 * is not a layout, it is a word-per-line. With both halves the sweep is 0 lost
 * everywhere out to a 40px root — at each card's BOTTOM clip edge, which is all that sweep
 * measured. NAME THE EDGE, always: the right edge was not zero, and this revision made it
 * worse rather than better before a later one closed it. Putting the control box in rem let
 * two controls plus their gap exceed a narrow card, which pushed the intro card's copy
 * column wider than the card and sheared the hero text. Measured on the right edge at a
 * 40px root: 136.84 lost at 320px wide, 96.84 at 360, 81.84 at 375, 42.84 at 414, against
 * 34.58 at 320px and nothing elsewhere before; at a 32px root — 200% text zoom — 47.44 at
 * 320px and 7.44 at 360px, where the previous revision was clean. Freeing the height budget
 * does not free the width: a card grows vertically when its content does and never
 * horizontally, because it is as wide as the viewport allows.
 *
 * Closed now, and closed differently for the two quantities involved — which are worth
 * keeping apart, because quoting one of them as "nothing clips" is the error that let this
 * ship. The control's box stayed text-relative and the control row stopped counting columns
 * at all — it wraps, so its minimum width is one control however large the reader's text;
 * BasicLayout.astro owns that argument. CONTROL BOXES sheared past the clip
 * edge at seven configurations, up to 142 at 320px and a 40px root, and are now 0 at every
 * one — that is the functional half, and no text-node sweep could see it. TEXT ink goes to
 * 0 at five of its six; the sixth, 320px at a 40px root, drops 136.84 -> 34.58, which is
 * one hero word against the card's own padding spending 120 of the 320 rather than the
 * control row. That residual predates all of this and is untouched by reverting the box to
 * device pixels.
 *
 * ---
 *
 * Two figures that are easy to quote wrongly. The three cards' gaps in the
 * intermediate state (sized to content, still in the shared row template) measure
 * 70px and 70px at 1440x900 — EQUAL, so the case for the stack is the size of those
 * gaps against the page's 16px, not any unevenness between them. And the leftover
 * under the last card is 106px at that viewport; the stack's unspent height is 138px,
 * of which 32px is the two gaps that are actually spent between the cards.
 */
---

<Layout
        title={METADATA.title}
        description={METADATA.description}
>
    <main
            class="text-[var(--text)] m-auto p-2 grid gap-2 max-w-6xl overflow-hidden relative w-full sm:p-4 md:grid-cols-2 md:gap-3 md:p-6 lg:grid-rows-[repeat(8,min-content)] lg:grid-cols-4 lg:gap-4 lg:min-h-[clamp(46rem,100vh,50rem)]"
    >
        <IntroCard/>
        {/* The lg grid is packed exactly 32/32 cells. Adding a goal card requires
             re-balancing the lg row/col spans in IntroCard/AboutMe/Now, otherwise
             auto-placement pushes Now into a zero-height implicit row and clips
             its content (see PR #42). */}
        {/* The right-hand column is its own stack, and it has to be. Grid rows are
             shared across every column, so the height of these three cards' rows is
             decided by the intro and role cards beside them, not by their own
             content: sized to content inside a shared row template they sat 70px
             apart, against the page's own 16px gap. As one grid item holding its own
             column, they stack at the page gap and the leftover collects once, under
             the last of them. This wrapper also re-orders siblings, which moves five
             cards below lg — measurements and the trade are in the frontmatter. */}
        <div class="contents lg:flex lg:flex-col lg:gap-4 lg:col-start-4 lg:row-start-1 lg:row-span-8">
            {GOALS.map((goal) => <Goal goal={goal}/>)}
            <Now/>
        </div>
        <AboutMe/>
        <Career/>
        {/* THE EXTRA lg ROWS ARE WHAT MAKE THE DATELINE FIT, and they are free.
             This card was one row holding a two-line paragraph. Its box already
             overflowed by 6px at 1440x900 and 14px at 1024x600 — which cuts NO ink,
             because that is the last line's leading and descender space inside 24px
             of padding, and the card still has 11-19px of real headroom. So the
             honest statement is not that a dateline makes an existing defect worse:
             it is that a dateline needs about 16px this card did not have. Without
             this row-span it loses actual glyphs at the DEFAULT 16px text size —
             5px at 1440x900, and 13px at every viewport whose height puts `main`
             on its 46rem floor. Those two figures belong to different
             configurations and must not be swapped: attaching the 1440x900 number to the
             floor configs understates this fix by 8px.

             The rows cost nothing because the grid already computes TEN row tracks —
             the eight explicit ones plus two implicit zero-height rows created by
             the career cards' six-row spans — so each extra row spends only a 16px
             gap that was already being spent on nothing. Measured across 40
             configurations: 0 ink lost, and every other card byte-identical in x, y,
             width and height. The footer alone grows 76 -> 92px.

             IT IS THREE ROWS NOW, and the third is what pays for the content-sized row
             template above. At two rows this card is auto-placed into row 8 plus an
             implicit row worth 0px, so its area was 92px for 106px of content — the
             14px being cut was its own bottom padding, which is why no ink probe ever
             saw it. A fraction template hides that; a content-sized one has to charge
             the whole page for it, and the grid asked for 813px instead of 797. With
             the third row this card stops driving the template at all: 106px of
             content into an area of 106px where the grid is content-driven and 109px
             from an 800px-tall viewport up, and the page pays nothing. (An earlier
             draft said "105px into 108px"; 108 occurs at no real viewport.)

             The single-line property is load-bearing, not tidiness. A dateline long
             enough to wrap reopens the same 5px loss WITH the row-span in place; the
             footer's text column is 182px at 1024px wide, and the worst date the
             calendar can produce ("Updated 30 September 2026") measures 165.75px.
             tests/projection.test.ts pins that bound. */}
        <Card colSpan="md:col-span-1" rowSpan="md:row-span-1 lg:row-span-3">
            <p class="text-xs">
                {FOOTER.prefix} <span class="text-[var(--brand-ink)]"><span class={iconClass(FOOTER.icon)} aria-hidden="true"></span></span><span class="sr-only">love</span>{FOOTER.suffix}
            </p>
            {/* THE ONLY WAY INTO `/design`, WHICH IS WHY IT IS NOT INSIDE THE SENTENCE ABOVE.
                 A build-wide gate walks the link graph from `/` and fails a page nothing
                 reaches, so this link is load-bearing rather than decorative. It sits on its
                 own line in the dateline's treatment because the paragraph above is an
                 attribution and this is a destination: running them together would need a
                 sentence break invented here, in a card whose copy is authored in
                 `src/content/`. `text-link` and the 1.5rem floor are what every other small
                 link on this site wears, and the words are `DESIGN_PAGE.link_label`, which is
                 the same constant that page is headed with.

                 IT COSTS 32px OF PAGE HEIGHT AND THERE WAS NO SLACK TO SPEND, so the number
                 in the budget note above moves: measured at 1024 wide on the built page,
                 against the live origin as the before-tree, `main` asks for 829px where it
                 asked for 797, and this card grows 106 to 138. The left column is what sets
                 the page's height — on production the footer card's bottom edge sat at 773
                 with `main`'s own 24px of padding beneath it and nothing else — so every
                 pixel added here is a pixel the page grows by, whatever the right-hand stack
                 has spare. A reader at 800px tall now gets a scrollbar where they did not.

                 THE CHEAPER SPELLINGS WERE PRICED AND REJECTED, so do not "fix" this by
                 reaching for one. Running the link into the attribution wraps that paragraph
                 to another line and still costs 16px, while inventing the sentence break the
                 copy does not carry. Dropping `min-h-6` saves 8px by putting a control under
                 the 1.5rem target floor this codebase set for exactly these links. Moving the
                 link to `/patches` costs nothing and satisfies the reachability gate through
                 the wall — and puts the site's colophon on a page about bike races. The 32px
                 buys the link its correct home; that is the trade, and it is the owner's to
                 reverse. */}
            <p class="text-xs mt-2">
                <a href="/design" class="text-link inline-flex items-center min-h-6 text-xs">{DESIGN_PAGE.link_label}</a>
            </p>
            {dateline && (
                    <p class="text-xs mt-2">Updated <time datetime={UPDATED_AT}>{dateline}</time></p>
            )}
        </Card>
    </main>
</Layout>

